{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261123T142904Z-0bd6c78e", "ts": "2026-11-23T14:32:47Z", "actor": {"worktree": "prawduct", "plugin": "3.9.1-dev", "branch": "fix/round-budget-cadence", "build": "95308783037f"}, "body": {"base_commit": "e07463047f13c73e21c665e8f8a5f363340dc94e", "base_tree": "c4421aace0c620e7f4da837f244ad9fd871bb6b8", "head_tree": "79852868e4b8ba04b9095fb12464d3d11ee97e13", "head_commit": "3029b5c1a29139399db7552b19e14b5d985bb4c3", "dispatch_commit": "3029b5c1a29139399db7552b19e14b5d985bb4c3", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["documentation/consumer-build-metrics.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md", "plugin/templates/project-state.yaml", "tests/test_critic_consolidate.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/changes/round-budget-cadence.md", "documentation/consumer-build-metrics.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md", "plugin/templates/project-state.yaml", "tests/test_critic_consolidate.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/changes/round-budget-cadence.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "The boundary allowance goes to the first whole-plan review that names no new chunk, wherever it falls in the plan. A mid-plan final re-review takes it, and the real boundary review is refused at the ceiling again", "recommendation": "`_cadence_round_ids` gives the boundary slot to the first `final`/`cumulative` with `named - reviewed` empty. It does not check where that review sits in the plan. A proposal only names open chunks (`plan_status.chunk_proposal`), so re-reviewing an already-certified chunk in `final` mode names nothing. That re-review is a loop round, and it is likely when a plan declares `Critic mode: final` and the builder re-runs `final` after fixes. The walk awards it the boundary allowance. At the ceiling the incoming check even waves it through, since `incoming_is_cadence` is true while `boundary_seen` is still false. After that, the real PR-boundary `cumulative` has `boundary_seen=True`, is charged, and is refused with exit 4. That is the defect this branch exists to fix, and it contradicts the change record's 'a planned review still runs after loops have used up the budget'. The same predicate errs the other way too. In a short plan or a `cumulative-final` plan, the boundary `cumulative` names open chunks, so it is booked as those chunks' steps. A second `cumulative` naming nothing then gets the boundary slot free. That contradicts the record's 'a second boundary review ... still counted' and project-state-keys.md's 'Every round after a chunk's first is counted'. This is the second fix of one class in two rounds (R-2 of rev-20261123T135555Z-05b709ab was the first). In core.md's words, 'A review finding names an instance; the defect is a class'. The class is that the boundary allowance is placed by mode and by what a review names, not by the plan step it serves. Fix it through one owner: a review that already claimed a chunk step also claims the boundary when it is a whole-plan review covering the last open chunk. Grant the slot only to a whole-plan review dispatched once the plan has no open chunk left. Add tests for (a) chunk 02 declared `final` and reviewed twice, then a boundary `cumulative` at the ceiling, which must dispatch, and (b) a short plan's boundary `cumulative` naming every chunk, then a second `cumulative`, which must be charged.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "tests/test_critic_consolidate.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "A chunk's first review dispatched the way the critic skill instructs (no --chunks) is still refused at the ceiling, its findings are swept first, and the skill's exit-4 row says stop, so the new --chunks remedy is never reached", "recommendation": "The incoming check sees only `declared_chunks`. `plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md` step 4 tells the dispatcher to 'Omit the flag to name the proposal', so the default chunk-review dispatch reaches `_round_budget_verdict` with `chunks: []` and is refused, even for a chunk's first review. `_refuse_over_budget` then auto-ACCEPTs every outstanding non-blocking finding in scope and records a guard-refusal firing. Only after that does `cmd_critic_begin` print the new 'name it with `--chunks <id>`' hint. Meanwhile SKILL.md's exit-4 row says 'paste stdout's census ... and stop', and that row is the 'exactly one correct response' the skill binds the agent to. So the hint's remedy is unreachable on the skill path, and the consumer note's 'a planned review still runs after loops have used up the budget' holds only for a builder who already passes --chunks. Two fixes would make the change complete. First, judge the incoming dispatch on its proposal: the `chunks-ambiguous` refusal already runs after `_dispatch_proposal` and before the manifest is written, which is the pattern to follow. Second, if the check has to stay blind, do not sweep or record a firing when the incoming dispatch is an undeclared `chunk` review. In either case, update SKILL.md's exit-4 row (and step 4) so a chunk's first review is re-dispatched with --chunks before stopping.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "The boundary step is still worked out from mode and chunk novelty, so the boundary allowance lands on the wrong review in three plan shapes the methodology documents", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why it broke: `_cadence_round_ids` never models which plan step a review serves. It works it out from a mode, a set of chunks already named and a `boundary_seen` flag, and 'boundary' means 'a whole-plan-mode review that names no new chunk'. That predicate catches a chunk re-review and misses a real boundary review. (1) A plan with `Critic mode: final` chunks: once chunk 02's first `final` is recorded, a second `final --chunks 02` (a loop round) names nothing new and boundary_seen is still False, so it is classed as the first boundary review. At the ceiling it passes as cadence. After that the real `cumulative` is charged and refused with exit 4, which is the failure this change exists to fix. The R-2 fix in the earlier round fixed a chunk's FIRST review in final mode, not its re-review. (2) A `Type: cumulative-final` plan, or a short plan, whose one cumulative names chunks no earlier review named: it is classed as those chunks' step and never claims the boundary slot, so the next cumulative (a loop round) is free as well. This is the second fix of this class in two rounds (core.md: 'A review finding names an instance; the defect is a class... Tell: your second fix of one class in two rounds'), so fix it through the one owner. Classify each full round once, as chunk-step, boundary-step or loop. Make the boundary step the first whole-plan review that comes after, or itself covers, every chunk it names, rather than one that 'names nothing new'. Or key it on the module's own stage model (`STAGE_OF_MODE`/`stage_of`, where only `cumulative` is the boundary stage) instead of `plan_status._WHOLE_PLAN_MODES`, which counts `final` as a boundary. Add tests for a `final`-mode chunk re-review before the boundary, and for a cumulative-final plan's second cumulative.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "tests/test_critic_consolidate.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "The budget check judges the incoming dispatch from different inputs than its recorded fact, and patches the gap with advice the skill driving the dispatch contradicts", "recommendation": "Scope: instance (one ordering in `begin_review`). Why it broke: `_round_budget_verdict(incoming=...)` runs before `_dispatch_proposal`, so the incoming dispatch is judged with `chunks = declared_chunks or []`, but its fact records `declared_chunks = chunks_proposed` when nothing was declared. The same review is judged on one input at dispatch and on another later, when history is walked. The change works around this by printing 'name it with `--chunks <id>`' on every exit-4 refusal and repeating the --chunks caveat in project-state-keys.md, api-contract.md and the change record. But `skills/critic/SKILL.md` step 4 says 'Omit the flag to name the proposal', and its exit-4 row says 'paste stdout's census ... and stop'. So on the skill's default path, a chunk's first review at the ceiling is refused, the refusal's `auto_accept` sweep has already run, and the agent stops. The consumer note's 'a planned review still runs after loops have used up the budget' is then false. The hint is also printed when the refusal cannot be a chunk's first review: on a `cumulative`, or on `--chunks 01` (the test asserts it there). Move the budget check below `_dispatch_proposal`. The chunks-ambiguous refusal already sits there, 'before the manifest, the marker or the archive', so the incoming body would carry exactly the chunks its fact will carry. Then delete the unconditional hint and the --chunks caveats in the docs, rather than adding an exit-4 branch to SKILL.md. If the check cannot move, say why in `begin_review`, print the hint only when `mode_token == 'chunk' and declared_chunks is None`, and make SKILL.md's exit-4 row route that case to a re-dispatch.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "The new exit-4 remedy ('name it with --chunks') is printed only after the refusal has already swept the scope's findings and recorded a guard-refusal, and the exit-4 consumer (SKILL.md) still says stop", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why it broke: the exit-4 remedy changed at its producer (cmd_critic_begin stdout) and not at the step order it depends on or at the consumer that acts on exit 4. In cmd_critic_begin the new line 'If this is a chunk's first review, name it with `--chunks <id>` ... and is not refused' prints after _refuse_over_budget has already run dispositions.auto_accept over the scope's review_ids, which records every outstanding non-blocking finding as ACCEPTED with the reason 'round budget exhausted', and after it has appended a critic-dispatch-round-budget guard-refusal. It also follows the CLI's own 'The loop ends here.' So the case the hint covers is a chunk's first review dispatched without --chunks, which SKILL.md step 4 makes the default ('Omit the flag to name the proposal'). In that case the builder (a) is told by SKILL.md's exit-4 row to 'paste stdout's census ... and stop', so the hint never reaches anyone who acts on it, or (b) re-dispatches with --chunks, and the review runs while the scope's findings stay accepted under a budget-exhausted reason that was false for this dispatch, and the guard-refusal yield query counts a firing that was a cadence step. The change record and the code comment give the reason for the order ('known only once the proposal is drawn, after this refusal'), but neither records the alternative of drawing the chunks before the budget check. To fix it, classify the incoming dispatch before anything irreversible happens. Either feed the drawn proposal's chunks into _round_budget_verdict's `incoming`, which removes the hint entirely, or refuse a chunk-mode dispatch with no --chunks at the ceiling before any sweep or guard-refusal record, with the --chunks remedy as its only output. Then make SKILL.md's exit-4 row say the same thing. Members to check: SKILL.md's exit table, the api-contract.md 'critic-begin 4' paragraph, and review-cycle.md's round-budget paragraph. Learnings rule: 'A mechanism change is done when every artifact DESCRIBING it agrees'.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "fid": "R-5"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 5, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "project-state-keys.md says a cadence dispatch at the ceiling runs 'when it names its chunks with --chunks', but the first boundary review runs naming none", "recommendation": "Reword it to match api-contract.md, which says 'named by `--chunks` or a first boundary review'. That way a reader does not conclude that a boundary review needs `--chunks` to get past the ceiling.", "files": ["plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "Governing-Artifact Reconciliation: no governed_by disposition to verify", "recommendation": "No live plan claims this branch (scope_unresolved_cause: unbound), and the change record carries no governed_by. There is no recorded disposition to check against the diff. The norm change the branch makes, which narrows what the round budget counts, is authorized by the 2026-11-23 owner ruling recorded in .prawduct/owner-rulings.md, items 2 and 'Merge when ready', including the transcription correction. The implementation matches the approved words.", "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "project-state-keys.md makes --chunks a condition for every cadence dispatch passing the ceiling, but the first boundary review passes without it", "recommendation": "'At the ceiling a dispatch that is itself one of them still runs, when it names its chunks with `--chunks`' covers both kinds of cadence step, but only a chunk's first review needs the flag. If the second finding moves the check, delete the clause. Otherwise make it say which kind needs the flag.", "files": ["plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "Shipped docstring cites a review finding id", "recommendation": "`_cadence_round_ids`'s docstring says 'answering R-9(a) of the plan that shipped the budget'. Finding ids do not belong in shipped code, so delete the clause and keep the owner-ruling citation. Wording only; nothing reads it.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "The incoming dispatch is classified by re-running the whole walk with a sentinel id appended", "recommendation": "`_round_budget_verdict` calls `_cadence_round_ids` twice, the second time over `[*in_scope, _INCOMING_ROUND]`, and needs a module-level sentinel that no minted id can match. If the walker returned its final state (the chunks it has seen and boundary_seen), or classified one more body, the incoming dispatch could be classified without the sentinel. This is small and local, so make the change only if the first finding's reshaping of the walker touches this code anyway.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "A cadence dispatch that passes an exhausted ceiling leaves no trace at dispatch", "recommendation": "_round_budget_verdict returns status 'within' when spent >= budget and incoming_is_cadence holds, and begin_review adds no note. The builder is not told that the loop budget is already spent, so the next non-cadence round is refused without warning. Nothing records that this round went past the ceiling as a plan step. The split can be recomputed later from the store with _cadence_round_ids, so this costs debuggability, not correctness. One PRAWDUCT NOTE line ('round budget spent (N/B); this dispatch runs as the plan's <chunk|boundary> step') would make the path visible.", "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "The shipped _cadence_round_ids docstring cites a finding id and its history", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. '(owner ruling 2026-11-23, answering R-9(a) of the plan that shipped the budget)' names a review finding id and narrates history in a shipped docstring. The protocol rates this a deletion, not a rewording. The rule is stated in the docstring's next sentences, so the parenthetical can go without losing anything. Prose ceiling: no edit is needed for this round.", "oid": "O-7"}], "duration_seconds": 660, "scope": null, "scope_chosen_by": "none", "scope_unresolved_cause": "unbound", "scope_reason": "no live plan claims `fix/round-budget-cadence`", "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": [], "chunks_proposed": [], "base_reviewed": "e07463047f13c73e21c665e8f8a5f363340dc94e", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/changes/round-budget-cadence.md", "documentation/consumer-build-metrics.md", "plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md"], "chunk_graded": null, "chunks_graded": [], "plan_graded": null, "findings": [], "unchecked": [], "counts": {"chunk-ref-missing": null, "governed-by-gap": 0, "suite-total-claim": 0, "learnings-over-budget": 0, "learnings-budget-unreasoned": 0, "learnings-core-raise-unapproved": 0, "learnings-rule-too-long": 0, "learnings-rule-body": 0, "learnings-area-dead": 0, "change-fragment": 0, "retired-claim-survives": 0, "late-requirement-record": 0}}, "span": "full", "bounded_from": null, "span_reason": "base-moved", "dispatched_at": "2026-11-23T14:29:08Z"}}

